Dynamic user textures: a chain asset the caller overwrites every frame.

`register_user_texture` is content-keyed and uploads once, which is the
right shape for an image loaded off disk and the wrong one for a source
that produces a new frame sixty times a second. This adds three calls
beside it. `register_dynamic_texture` mints an id off the same counter
but records it separately, so it never collides with a hashed one and
the two can't be confused; `update_user_texture` replaces the pixels,
checking the byte count against the size the texture was made with;
`release_user_texture` frees it. A hashed id is refused by both of the
last two, because those textures are shared by content and nobody owns
them.

On blade a dynamic texture is the same texture and view a registered one
gets, plus an `initialised` flag: `init_texture` is the undefined-to-
general layout transition and wants running once, not once per copy, so
the flush loop does it on the first upload only. A second update between
two frames drops the first rather than uploading both. Release moves the
handles onto a retirement list drained at the end of the next `draw`,
after the wait that says the frame which might have been reading them is
done. On DirectX the usage becomes `DEFAULT` instead of `IMMUTABLE` and
the update is one `UpdateSubresource` on the device context; the entry
now keeps the `ID3D11Texture2D` beside its view for that, and release
just drops the entry, since D3D11 holds a resource alive until the
commands naming it retire.

Either way a chain that still binds a released id samples transparent
black instead of losing its draw: blade gets a 1x1 stand-in the way
`mask` already has one, DirectX leaves the slot unbound, which reads as
zeros in HLSL. A panel outliving its texture by a frame should go
blank, not flicker out.

`update_user_texture` takes the bytes as an `Arc<Vec<u8>>` rather than a
slice. A source publishing a frame at a time was paying two full-size
copies to get one frame on screen: one to hand the frame out of its own
slot, one for the queue in here to keep it until the flush. At a
megabyte a frame the copies were the smaller half of that; the fresh
allocation each one made was faulted in a page at a time on first
touch, which measured 40x the memcpy. Now the caller hands over a
handle, the flush drops it, and the source gets its buffer back.
DirectX moves by signature only: it still keeps its own copy for the
device-lost rebuild, and none of that half has ever been compiled here.

